    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight mb-4 text-white">
      Stair Stringer Throat Depth: The 3.5-Inch Code Rule
    </h1>

    <p class="text-amber-100 text-base sm:text-lg max-w-3xl leading-relaxed mb-6">
      When you cut triangular notches into a stringer for stair steps, you remove up to 60% of the board's solid structural wood. Under <strong>IRC Section R311.7.5.3</strong>, the remaining uncut diagonal backbone—known as the <strong>throat depth (or effective depth)</strong>—must measure at least <strong>3.5 inches (5 inches in many local coastal/seismic codes)</strong>. Here is the mathematical proof of why 2x10 lumber fails code and why 2x12s are mandatory.
    </p>

    <!-- Technical Infographic Hero Diagram -->
    <div class="bg-stone-900/80 border border-amber-500/30 rounded-xl p-5 max-w-3xl">
      <h3 class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-300 font-bold mb-3">Throat Depth Geometry: 2x12 vs 2x10 Analysis</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-center">
        <div class="bg-stone-950/80 p-4 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/30">
          <span class="text-3xl font-black text-emerald-400">5.0 to 5.5 in</span>
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-white mt-1">2x12 Lumber (11.25 in Actual)</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-stone-300 mt-2">Leaves a generous 5-inch throat after cutting a 7.5-in rise and 10.5-in run. <strong>100% Code Compliant.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-stone-950/80 p-4 rounded-lg border border-red-500/30">
          <span class="text-3xl font-black text-red-400">2.5 to 3.0 in</span>
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-white mt-1">2x10 Lumber (9.25 in Actual)</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-stone-300 mt-2">Leaves under 3 inches of throat. <strong>Violates IRC R311.7.5.3</strong> and risks catastrophic mid-span failure.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Content -->
  <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-6 mb-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">How to Calculate Stringer Throat Depth Mathematically</h2>
    <p>
      You do not have to guess your throat depth. You can calculate it mathematically using the Pythagorean theorem before making a single saw cut:
    </p>

    <div class="bg-gray-100 p-5 rounded-xl font-mono text-sm border border-gray-300">
      <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">The Stair Throat Formula:</p>
      <p>1. Notch Hypotenuse = &radic;(Unit Rise&sup2; + Unit Run&sup2;)</p>
      <p>2. Notch Depth to Corner = (Unit Rise &times; Unit Run) &divide; Hypotenuse</p>
      <p>3. Throat Depth = Actual Lumber Width &minus; Notch Depth to Corner</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-emerald-800 font-semibold">Example for 7.25-in Rise & 10.5-in Run on a 2x12 (11.25-in Actual):</p>
      <p>Hypotenuse = &radic;(7.25&sup2; + 10.5&sup2;) = &radic;(52.56 + 110.25) = 12.76 inches</p>
      <p>Notch Depth = (7.25 &times; 10.5) &divide; 12.76 = 76.125 &divide; 12.76 = 5.96 inches</p>
      <p>Throat Depth = 11.25 &minus; 5.96 = <strong>5.29 Inches (&gt; 3.5-inch Code Minimum)</strong></p>
    </div>

    <p>
      If you perform this exact same calculation on a 2x10 (9.25-inch actual width):<br />
      Throat Depth = 9.25 &minus; 5.96 = <strong>3.29 Inches</strong>. This is <em>under the 3.5-inch legal threshold</em> and will fail inspection every single time.
    </p>

    <!-- Comparative Table -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">Lumber Sizing & Center Spacing Rules</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose my-6">
